From 8e23577a71024403267debb9e9b6823ac1d78e1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:30:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] server: end generation cleanly on a tool-call-diff
 inconsistency

Root cause: common_chat_msg_diff::compute_diffs() throws when a later,
fuller reparse of the accumulated generation legitimately finds fewer tool
calls than an earlier partial reparse speculatively recognized (the earlier
parse guessed a tool-call shape that more text later retracts). This is a
real, non-buggy outcome of incremental parsing, but task_result_state::
update_chat_msg() calls compute_diffs() once per streamed chunk (re-parsing
the whole accumulated text each time) with no way to recover from the
throw, so it propagates uncaught all the way to server-http.cpp's fail-safe
exception handler, turning a well-formed streaming completion into a raw
500 mid-stream. Downstream, LiteLLM's OpenAI-compatible client surfaces
this as `APIConnectionError: ... Invalid diff: now finding less tool
calls!`, and its order-based-fallback retry logic discards the whole
generation and retries from scratch on the next tier every time this
happens.

Reproduction: confirmed live via LiteLLM/opencode against this fork's
production llama-server, and independently reproduced locally --
`tests/test-chat.cpp`'s `test_template_output_peg_parsers()` already hits
this exact condition (uncaught, crashing test-chat) against
models/templates/Qwen3-Coder.jinja's `html` tool: a partial reparse
speculatively opens an `html` tool call that a fuller reparse retracts.
Confirmed via `git stash` that this crash pre-exists on fleet-patches,
unrelated to and unaffected by this fix (it calls compute_diffs() directly,
bypassing update_chat_msg() below) -- the parser's own false-positive tool-
call detection is a separate, deeper problem, not addressed here.

Fix: give compute_diffs()'s throw a distinguishable exception type
(common_chat_msg_diff_invalid_error, deriving from std::runtime_error so
existing generic catches still work) instead of a bare std::runtime_error.
update_chat_msg() catches it specifically, rolls back to the last
successfully-diffed message, and sets a new diff_error_stop flag instead of
propagating. server_response_reader::next() (the single chokepoint every
streaming route funnels through) checks that flag after update() returns:
cancels the task's remaining generation via the existing stop() mechanism
(same as any other early termination -- no further tokens are generated
for the request past this point), and forces a still-streaming partial
result to report is_stop() so the normal end-of-stream bookkeeping
(received_count, [DONE]) fires unchanged. For the OAI chat-completions
format (the reported/reproduced path), cmpl_partial::to_json_oaicompat_
chat() emits one extra finish_reason="stop" delta so the client sees a
normal, well-formed completion instead of a dropped stream. Every
content/tool-call delta already sent before the inconsistency was detected
is untouched.

Scope note: the Responses (/v1/responses) and Anthropic Messages
(/v1/messages) streaming formats are also chat/tool-call-capable and can
hit the same underlying inconsistency, but their SSE lifecycles are
event-based (response.output_item.done, message_stop, etc.) rather than a
single finish_reason field; this fix does not add a bespoke graceful-finish
event for them. They still get the crash-free, generation-stopped behavior
(via the same diff_error_stop / stop() path), just without an explicit
in-band finish event -- the stream simply ends. Not fixed here for scope
reasons; flagged in case it's worth closing later.

Added a unit test in test_msg_diffs_compute() (tests/test-chat.cpp)
constructing a msg_prv with more tool_calls than msg_new, confirming
compute_diffs() throws common_chat_msg_diff_invalid_error specifically (not
just any std::runtime_error) and that the existing growth-diff assertions
in the same function are unaffected.

